For the radial coordinate, $\pi_r=\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial\dot r}=m\dot r,$ and hence $d\pi_r/dt=m\ddot r$. There are two contributions to $\partial\mathcal L/\partial r$: one from the potential and one from the factor $r^2$ in the angular kinetic energy. Thus $\begin{aligned}
 \frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial r}
 &=\frac{\partial}{\partial r}
   \left(\frac{m}{2}r^2\dot\theta^{\,2}-U(r)\right) \\
 &=mr\dot\theta^{\,2}-\frac{dU}{dr}.\end{aligned}$ The radial equation is therefore $m\ddot r=mr\dot\theta^{\,2}-\frac{dU}{dr}.$

