\begin{aligned}
 \delta A
 &={}
 \int_{t_1}^{t_2}\sum_i
 \left[
 \frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial q_i}
 -\frac{d}{dt}\left(
   \frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial\dot q_i}
  \right)
 \right]\delta q_i\,dt \\
 &\quad+
 \left[
 \sum_i\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial\dot q_i}\,
 \delta q_i
 \right]_{t_1}^{t_2}.
 \end{aligned}

p.src:
For a rotation in the $x$-$y$ plane, use Eq. . For one particle, $\begin{aligned}
 Q_{\mathrm{rot}}
 &=p_x(-y)+p_yx \\
 &=xp_y-yp_x.
 \end{aligned}$ This is the component of $\mathbf r\times\mathbf p$ perpendicular to the plane. For many particles, $L_z=\sum_a\left(x_ap_{y_a}-y_ap_{x_a}\right).$ Rotations about the other axes give the other components of the angular momentum vector.

