en.math:
\begin{aligned}
 H
 &=\pi_x\dot x-\mathcal L \\
 &=m\dot x^2-\left(\frac12m\dot x^2-U(x)\right) \\
 &=\frac12m\dot x^2+U(x).
 \end{aligned}

p.src:
Not every arbitrary function is equally useful as a Lagrangian. At this stage, two conditions are worth recording. It should depend locally on the coordinates and velocities, and the momenta $\pi_i=\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial\dot q_i}$ should normally determine the velocities. Locally, the standard regularity condition is $\det\left(
 \frac{\partial^2\mathcal L}
 {\partial\dot q_i\,\partial\dot q_j}
 \right)\neq0.$ This is what permits the passage from Lagrangian to Hamiltonian variables. A full treatment belongs to Hamiltonian mechanics.

